        "root_concepts": "[pragmatics]",
        "section": "philosophy-of-language",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Pragmatics: Grice and Implicature",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Semantics deals with what sentences MEAN. Pragmatics deals with what speakers COMMUNICATE — which is typically much more than what sentences literally say. The gap between sentence meaning (semantic content) and speaker meaning (what's communicated in context) is the domain of pragmatics."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-PRAGMATICS.md#003",
      "path": [
        "08-PRAGMATICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:philosophy-of-language:08-pragmatics, git-history:philosophy-of-language:08-pragmatics]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy-of-language/08-PRAGMATICS.md",
        "concepts": "[pragmatics]",
        "current_path": "philosophy-of-language/08-PRAGMATICS.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy-of-language:pragmatics",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy-of-language",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[pragmatics]",
        "section": "philosophy-of-language",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Pragmatics: Grice and Implicature",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "H.P. Grice (1913–1988) laid the foundations by identifying the inferential mechanisms by which hearers recover what speakers mean from what they say."
    },
    {
      "id": "08-PRAGMATICS.md#004",
      "path": [
        "08-PRAGMATICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:philosophy-of-language:08-pragmatics, git-history:philosophy-of-language:08-pragmatics]",
